<h1>Why MD5 Is Bad for Passwords, Even With a Salt</h1>
<p>MD5 should not be used for password storage in new systems. This is true even if the system adds a salt. A salt solves some problems, but it does not change the basic fact that MD5 is fast and not designed as a password hashing algorithm. Fast hashing is useful for files. It is a weakness when attackers are guessing passwords offline.</p>
<p>The problem starts after a database leak. If attackers obtain password hashes, they can test guesses on their own hardware. They do not need to use the login form. They can calculate MD5 hashes for candidate passwords and compare them with the leaked values. Because MD5 is fast, each guess is cheap.</p>
<p>A salt helps by making precomputed tables less useful and by ensuring that two users with the same password do not automatically have the same stored hash. OWASP's <a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html">Password Storage Cheat Sheet</a> explains why salts matter. But a salted MD5 hash can still be attacked by trying guesses one by one with the known salt.</p>
<p>Modern password hashing algorithms add another layer: cost. Bcrypt has a work factor. Argon2id can use memory and time parameters. PBKDF2 uses many iterations. These controls make each guess slower. The goal is not to make login painfully slow for real users. The goal is to make millions or billions of guesses expensive for attackers.</p>
<p>MD5 also carries a separate security history. It has known collision weaknesses, and <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6151">RFC 6151</a> updated the security considerations for MD5. Collision weakness is not the same as password guessing, but it reinforces the broader point: MD5 is a legacy algorithm, not a modern password storage choice.</p>
<p>Developers sometimes keep MD5 because old systems used it, examples are easy to find, or database fields are already sized for a 32-character string. Those are understandable reasons for legacy maintenance, but they are not good reasons to keep the design permanently. A password table is one of the most important places to modernize.</p>
<p>The safest migration pattern is usually gradual. Keep existing MD5 hashes only long enough to verify the next successful login. When the user logs in correctly, rehash the submitted password with bcrypt, Argon2id, or another approved method, then replace the old hash. This avoids needing to know the user's plaintext password in advance.</p>
<p>If you need to persuade non-technical stakeholders, focus on attack economics. The issue is not that every MD5 hash is instantly known. The issue is that MD5 lets attackers test guesses cheaply after a leak. Better password hashing raises the cost of each guess. That gives users and site owners more protection when something else has already gone wrong.</p>
<p>A migration plan should also consider user communication, backups, and support access. If password resets are needed, make the process clear and short. Do not email passwords, do not ask support agents to view passwords, and do not temporarily save plaintext values for convenience. Those shortcuts create new risks.</p>
<p>The conclusion is direct. Salted MD5 is better than unsalted MD5, but it is still not good enough for current password storage. Use a real password hashing algorithm. If you need background on the difference between hash types, read <a href="/password-hashing-vs-encryption.html">Password Hashing vs Encryption</a> before changing production code.</p>
